# RESERVED — names kit has retired, and will not use again.
#
# ONE file, in kit, for the whole fleet, in the same dialect as
# `templates/tier/skip-allowlist` and `templates/parity-allowlist`: the same four
# rules, the same words, one entry per line. Two dialects of "record why" is how
# one of them goes stale.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#
# A tombstone. When kit retires something a service may hold — a template path,
# a copied file, a name — the removal records the name here so that name can
# never be used for something else.
#
# WHY, WHEN KIT HAS NO WIRE FORMAT
#
# buf's `reserved` and Kubernetes' `+k8s:deprecated=width,protobuf=3` are the two
# references, and **they are not the same mechanism**, which is worth more than
# a merge of the two would be:
#
#   * buf's `reserved: 3, 7;` is a WIRE tombstone. The field number is what
#     travels in the bytes, so the rule is that a retired number may not be
#     reused by a new field — otherwise an old message and a new one decode the
#     same bytes into two different meanings. Its unit is an identifier in an
#     encoding, and its whole purpose is to make DELETION non-breaking.
#   * Kubernetes' is a LIFECYCLE marker on a field that is still there. k8s does
#     not delete an API; it deprecates it, names the replacement and the version,
#     and leaves the type in place. `reserved: 3` appears in the generated Go as
#     the *matching* tombstone, but the mechanism it carries is "deprecated, not
#     removed".
#
# kit has no encoding, so buf's justification does not transfer — there is no
# byte whose meaning a reused name would corrupt. What DOES transfer, and what
# both references actually enforce, is the weaker and the real half: **a retired
# name stays retired.** It is not about the wire. It is about the fact that kit
# is consumed by COPY, so a service's copy of a retired path outlives the
# decision, and a path that comes back under the same name is read by that
# service as "kit restored it" when in fact it is a different thing entirely.
#
# So this file adopts the tombstone, and says plainly that it is NOT the
# deprecation-instead-of-removal mechanism. Two reasons that one does not port:
# a comment in a copied YAML file is a comment in a copy — the adopter's file
# never learns it — and kit has no registry a deprecation could live in, since
# the artefact is fetched by ref or copied by hand. `templates/parity-allowlist`
# already carries the deprecation half for the fleet: it says which copies have
# diverged from kit's bytes and why, and the entry has to keep matching.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR HYGIENE RULES, AND WHERE THE FOURTH ONE IS INVERTED
#
# 1. EVERY ENTRY NAMES A REASON.
#    Without one, "we removed it" becomes the reason for everything, and a
#    reason that means nothing cannot be reviewed, expired, or argued with.
#
# 2. EVERY ENTRY NAMES AN OWNER.
#    A tombstone nobody owns is a tombstone that gets deleted by whoever next
#    tidies the tree. The owner is a person or a team, not a distribution list.
#
# 3. EVERY ENTRY NAMES THE VERSION IT DIED IN.
#    `retired_in` is the version at which the name was already gone, which is
#    what makes the entry checkable: an entry retiring a name in a version
#    LATER than VERSION is asserting a removal that has not happened yet.
#
#    THERE IS NO `until`, and that is the one deliberate difference from the
#    other two allowlists. There, an entry that cannot expire has stopped being
#    a decision and become a fact. Here that is the POINT: a tombstone does not
#    expire. Deleting the entry is the only thing that ends it, and that is a
#    different act — reintroducing the name — which is what the fourth rule
#    catches.
#
# 4. AN ENTRY THAT MATCHES NOTHING IS A FAILURE — INVERTED.
#    For a skip allowlist, an entry that matches nothing is a ratchet that only
#    turns one way. For a tombstone the dead entry and the live name are the
#    same defect with two names: **the entry says the name is gone and the name
#    is in the tree.** So `reserved_check` fails when a reserved path EXISTS.
#    That is the whole check, and it is the direction a reader would not
#    guess: nothing here permits anything. The gate is red when a retired name
#    comes back, and silent otherwise.
#
# THE TOTAL IS PRINTED, PROMENENTLY, ON EVERY RUN.
#
# Two entries, which is a good number — the honest measure of a mechanism nobody
# has needed yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE `kind` COLUMN
#
# `path` today, and it is checked: the named path must not exist in the tree.
# An unknown kind is a FAILURE rather than a shrug, because a kind nobody
# implements is an entry that reads as protection and provides none. Adding a
# kind means adding its check in the same commit — the same rule the rest of
# this repository follows for a new file type.

reserved path templates/backup/ retired_in=1.0.0 reason="kit-20 built a backup distribution and then deleted it because kamal-backup is that tool; the directory is retired so it cannot come back as a second implementation of a command surface that already exists" owner=kit since=2026-10-03
reserved path templates/bin/backup.sh retired_in=1.0.0 reason="the copied entry point of the same removed distribution; a file at this path is a service that adopted the build that was withdrawn, and the tombstone is what says the path was never empty" owner=kit since=2026-10-03